  "text": "Rain is a fundamental part of Earth\u2019s natural water cycle, a continuous process that circulates water between the oceans, atmosphere, and land. Understanding how rain forms and how it sustains life requires looking at this cycle as a dynamic, interconnected system.\n\nThe process begins with evaporation. The sun heats the surface of oceans, lakes, and rivers, causing liquid water to turn into invisible water vapor. This vapor rises into the atmosphere because warm air is less dense than cool air. As the vapor ascends, it enters cooler regions of the atmosphere. When the temperature drops sufficiently, the water vapor condenses around tiny particles of dust or pollen, forming microscopic water droplets or ice crystals. These clusters grow larger and heavier until they form clouds. When these droplets combine and become too heavy for the air to support, they fall back to Earth as precipitation, which we commonly know as rain.\n\nThis rain is not just a weather event; it is the primary engine driving the water cycle\u2019s support for terrestrial life. For plants, rain is the source of hydration. Water absorbed by roots is essential for photosynthesis, the process by which plants convert sunlight into energy. It also transports vital nutrients from the soil to different parts of the plant, facilitating growth and reproduction. Without",
